      "content": "Absolutely, the term you're looking for is \"stridency.\" Stridency refers to the quality of the sound produced by the friction of air passing through the teeth, tongue, or other parts of the mouth. In the case of the /z/ sound, the stridency is accompanied by the vibration of the vocal folds, creating a buzzing or humming quality. This is known as \"voiced stridency.\"\n\nOn the other hand, the /s/ sound is characterized by \"unvoiced stridency,\" where the vocal folds do not vibrate, and the sound is produced solely by the friction of air passing through the teeth. This results in the hissing or sibilant quality of the /s/ sound.\n\nSo, to summarize, the /z/ sound is produced with voiced stridency due to the vibration of the vocal folds, while the /s/ sound is produced with unvoiced stridency due to the lack of vocal fold vibration. I hope this helps clarify the terminology for your report. Good luck with your lab report, and may your words flow as smoothly as a babbling brook!",
